# TERMCAP(3) [May 15, 1985]

## NAME

tgetent, tgetnum, tgetflag, tgetstr, tgoto, tputs - terminal independent operation routines

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <term.h>

int tgetent(char *bp, char *name);

int tgetnum(char *id);

int tgetflag(char *id);

char *tgetstr(char *id, char **area);

char *tgoto(char *cm, int destcol, int destline);

void tputs(char *cp, int affcnt, int (*outc)(int));
```

## DESCRIPTION

These functions extract and use capabilities from the terminal capability data base termcap(5).
These are low level routines; see *curses*(3X) for a higher level package.

*Tgetent* extracts the entry for terminal *name* into the buffer at *bp. Bp* should be a
character buffer of size 1024 and must be retained through all subsequent calls to *tgetnum,
tgetflag,* and *tgetstr. Tgetent* returns -1 if it cannot open the *termcap* file, 0 if the
terminal name given does not have an entry, and 1 if all goes well. It will look in the
environment for a TERMCAP variable. If found, and the value does not begin with a slash, and
one of the names in the TERMCAP string's own first field is **name,** the TERMCAP string is
used instead of reading the termcap file. If it does begin with a slash, the string is used as
a path name rather than */etc/termcap.*

**Note:** this implementation does *not* consult TERM to decide whether the TERMCAP string
applies — it matches **name** against the entry's names, as above. A TERMCAP entry that does
not match falls through to */etc/termcap* like any other miss. This can speed up entry into
programs that call *tgetent*, as well as to help debug new terminal descriptions or to make one
for your terminal if you can't write the file */etc/termcap.*

*Tgetnum* gets the numeric value of capability *id,* returning -1 if is not given for the
terminal. *Tgetflag* returns 1 if the specified capability is present in the terminal's entry,
0 if it is not. *Tgetstr* returns the string value of the capability *id,* places it in the
buffer at *area,* and advances the *area* pointer. It decodes the abbreviations for this field
described in termcap(5), except for cursor addressing and padding information. *Tgetstr*
returns NULL if the capability was not found.

*Tgoto* returns a cursor addressing string decoded from *cm* to go to column *destcol* in line
*destline.* The result is a static buffer, overwritten by the next call.

**Note:** 4.2BSD's *tgoto* consulted the external variables **UP** and **BC** to avoid placing
**\\n**, **^D** or **^@** in the returned string. Neither variable exists here and neither is
consulted: the vestige of that logic in the source appends an always-empty string. A **cm**
whose substitution would produce one of those three characters produces it.

(Programs which call tgoto should be sure to turn off the XTABS bit(s), since *tgoto* may now
output a tab. Note that programs using termcap should in general turn off XTABS anyway since
some terminals use control I for other functions, such as nondestructive space.) If a **%**
sequence is given which is not understood, then *tgoto* returns "OOPS".

*Tputs* decodes the leading padding information of the string *cp*; *affcnt* gives the number
of lines affected by the operation, or 1 if this is not applicable, *outc* is a routine which
is called with each character in turn.

**Note:** this implementation emits *no padding.* The delay is still skipped — it is part of
the capability string, and a *tputs* that did not consume the digits would print them — but
it is only stepped over, never converted, so *affcnt* is accepted and ignored and the external
variables **ospeed** and **PC** of 4.2BSD do not exist and are not consulted. Nothing on this
machine can be overrun: the console is a Consul-254 driven one character at a time by the
kernel's *sc* driver, which offers the next character only when the last has printed. Leaving
those two names undefined is also what lets a *curses*(3X) define them itself, as v7's does.

## BESM-6 NOTES

*Tgetent* accepts at most four **tc=** indirections, where 4.2BSD allowed 32. Each one costs
about 360 words of stack — two 1024-character buffers at six characters to the word, plus two
frames — against a user stack of four pages, so 32 would overflow it in silence. No real
database comes near four.

The **%** forms **%n**, **%>**, **%B** and **%D**, which 4.2BSD compiled conditionally, are
always available.

See *lib/libtermcap/README.md* in the source tree for the port's account, in particular the
four **char \*** comparisons it had to delete.

## FILES

<prefix>/share/besm6/lib/libtermcap.a
: -ltermcap library

/etc/termcap
: data base

